  "why": "The statement asks for 2n−2 containment for every n and every oriented tree. Kühn–Mycroft–Osthus settle all n ≥ n0 (a case, refereed in PLMS); Havet–Thomassé (arborescences) and Reid–Wormald (near-regular hosts) are restricted classes; the linear-constant papers (Häggkvist–Thomason, El Sahili, KMO approximate, Dross–Havet) are bounds. Mycroft–Naia and both Benford–Montgomery papers give asymptotic host orders for restricted tree classes or same-order unavoidability — different quantities from the 2n−2 threshold, so variant. The Burr-conjecture papers concern chromatic number with bounds weaker than known tournament bounds, and Aboulker et al. treat other digraph classes: variant. Ai–Lian bundles a bound (18/7 coefficient) and an exact 2n−2 result for the whole class of bi-arborescences (Lemma 3); the latter is a genuine new restriction settled at the conjectured value, so I scored it case rather than bound — either way unreviewed preprint, credible, no AI.",
